  "final_text": "I finished all four steps and checked each one by reading it back from Kibana.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\" (id `90943e30-9a47-11e8-b64d-95841ca0b247`, index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) has these fields:\n   - **Request:** `request`, `url`, `referer`, `response` (text, with a `response.keyword` version for exact matching), `bytes`, `extension`, `agent`, `host`, `tags`, `message`.\n   - **Location and network:** `clientip`, `ip`, `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.srcdest`, `geo.coordinates`.\n   - **Machine:** `machine.os`, `machine.ram`, `memory`, `phpmemory`.\n   - **Dates:** `@timestamp`, `timestamp`, `utc_time`, `hour_of_day`.\n   \n   The time field is **`timestamp`**. There is also an `@timestamp` field, but the data view doesn't use it.\n2. **Dashboard:** \"S4-6c6e56c8 Web logs\" (id `134a5b68-4ec7-4910-8608-840da243fb8e`) has exactly two panels, both counting requests:\n   - a line chart, \"Requests over time\", grouped by `timestamp`;\n   - a bar chart of the top 10 values of `response.keyword`.\n3. **Alert rule:** \"S4-6c6e56c8 5xx errors\" (id `7a021e63-7853-44c9-b5d9-25ccd838f1ed`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 matching requests arrive within 5 minutes.\n   - It counts a request as a server error when its response code is any three-digit value from 500 to 599.\n   - The response code is stored as text, so a plain numeric range wouldn't apply. I matched the pattern `5[0-9][0-9]` instead, which covers exactly 500\u2013599.\n   - The rule was created enabled, so it didn't need enabling.\n4. **Edit and read-back:** I renamed the response-code panel to \"Responses by code\", and the dashboard still has two panels. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true`, and its last run finished with status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
